trio to help you sharpen and check it.

5
00:00:25.289 --> 00:00:30.411
You’ll decide what can be ready at the
start, what will build across the term,

6
00:00:30.412 --> 00:00:35.466
and what you need to ask other people
about. Begin with the students you have

7
00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:40.788
in mind and the access questions on your
day map. What does the environment, task

8
00:00:40.789 --> 00:00:45.578
or routine ask of them? What already
helps? What might be clearer or more

9
00:00:45.579 --> 00:00:50.700
workable? The setup will be something to
try, review and refine with students,

10
00:00:50.701 --> 00:00:55.290
families and colleagues. Keep your
questions open and your support map

11
00:00:55.291 --> 00:00:58.683
available. Knowing who to ask is part of
the design.

12
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:05.029
Students come first. Before choosing a
strategy, return to the real students and

13
00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:09.369
the evidence on your day map. What matters
to them? What is the specific

14
00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:14.321
access question? What have you observed,
and what are you still wondering? Each of

15
00:01:14.322 --> 00:01:18.968
you will design a day-one setup for your
own classroom, then check it against

16
00:01:18.969 --> 00:01:23.859
different students. If you don’t have a
class yet, you can keep using a placement

17
00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:27.955
class or a class you know well. Keep an
observation separate from an

18
00:01:27.956 --> 00:01:32.784
interpretation, and a question separate
from a conclusion. If you don’t know how

19
00:01:32.785 --> 00:01:37.553
a student experiences something, leave
room to find out through consultation. A

20
00:01:37.554 --> 00:01:42.444
useful design brief connects what matters
for students with what we can change in

21
00:01:42.445 --> 00:01:46.723
the environment, task or routine. It keeps
individual knowledge in view

22
00:01:46.724 --> 00:01:49.658
without reducing a person to their support
needs.

23
00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:56.154
Early in your career, you’re choosing
classroom habits that may stay with you.

24
00:01:56.155 --> 00:02:00.955
This is a useful time to build access into
those habits. Some things can be

25
00:02:00.956 --> 00:02:06.210
prepared before a student has to ask for
them. Think about a schedule you refer to

26
00:02:06.211 --> 00:02:11.271
every day, a consistent cue for gaining
attention, or a transition routine that

27
00:02:11.272 --> 00:02:16.266
is explained and taught. A calm space can
be somewhere students choose to use,

28
00:02:16.267 --> 00:02:21.327
rather than a consequence or a place they
are sent. Predictability can help the

29
00:02:21.328 --> 00:02:26.063
teacher too, especially while you are
still learning how a school works. A

30
00:02:26.064 --> 00:02:31.123
clear starting routine means you and the
students have useful information about

31
00:02:31.124 --> 00:02:35.859
what will happen next. Day one doesn’t
have to mean the first day of a new

32
00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:40.854
school year. It can be the first day of
next term, your next placement or your

33
00:02:40.855 --> 00:02:45.461
next relief booking. For a relief teacher,
the setup might be the lesson

34
00:02:45.462 --> 00:02:49.807
plan written up at the start, an attention
cue and a clear finishing

35
00:02:49.808 --> 00:02:54.478
routine. You don’t need to set up
everything at once. As you listen, hold

36
00:02:54.479 --> 00:02:59.539
one question: what could be in place in my
classroom on day one that would make

37
00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:00.447
access easier?

38
00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:07.038
Some ideas about the perfect classroom
arrive with the job. We’ve seen them on

39
00:03:07.039 --> 00:03:12.116
placement, in colleagues’ rooms or in
resources. This slide invites us to look

40
00:03:12.117 --> 00:03:17.129
at those practices carefully. There is
plenty worth keeping: clear structure,

41
00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:21.812
warmth, explicitly taught routines and
high expectations. Those can help

42
00:03:21.813 --> 00:03:26.890
students understand the environment and
know they are welcome in it. Then look

43
00:03:26.891 --> 00:03:31.705
again at practices such as sticker charts,
public behaviour displays, very

44
00:03:31.706 --> 00:03:36.718
busy walls and routines that rely on
everyone responding in the same way. Ask

45
00:03:36.719 --> 00:03:41.929
what the practice is for, who can succeed
within it, and what it costs a student

46
00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:47.007
who cannot. A very busy wall may add
sensory demand throughout a day. A public

47
00:03:47.008 --> 00:03:52.020
display may make a student’s regulation
visible to the whole class. Those are

48
00:03:52.021 --> 00:03:57.033
reasons to examine how the practice is
experienced, including by the students

49
00:03:57.034 --> 00:04:02.111
for whom participation is hardest. Keep
this discussion respectful. Colleagues

50
00:04:02.112 --> 00:04:07.256
may use these systems thoughtfully and may
know context you haven’t encountered

51
00:04:07.257 --> 00:04:12.466
yet. Look at the practice and ask useful
questions. Separate what is required at

52
00:04:12.467 --> 00:04:17.611
your school from what is customary and
what you can influence in your own room.

53
00:04:17.612 --> 00:04:22.492
Where a decision sits beyond your role,
take the question to your mentor or

54
00:04:22.493 --> 00:04:27.372
leader. Return to the day map: which
habits support access, and which might

55
00:04:27.373 --> 00:04:27.966
add cost?

56
00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:33.815
Here is the rhythm for the rest of this
session. We’ve begun with two short

57
00:04:33.816 --> 00:04:38.444
inputs. Next we’ll form trios, and each
person will write their own brief. We’ll

58
00:04:38.445 --> 00:04:42.955
consider four support options, then apply
a universal design lens. After that,

59
00:04:42.956 --> 00:04:47.525
you’ll build your setup and privately
check it against the students you have in

60
00:04:47.526 --> 00:04:51.978
mind. That check will lead into the
continuum of support. We’ll finish with a

61
00:04:51.979 --> 00:04:56.607
gallery and a commitment. Your trio helps
you sharpen and check your work, while

62
00:04:56.608 --> 00:05:00.943
each person keeps their own setup and
students in view. You’ll leave with a

63
00:05:00.944 --> 00:05:05.513
day-one plan and an asking list. At each
activity, the recording leaves a brief

64
00:05:05.514 --> 00:05:09.556
pause. Pause the video for the full
working time whenever you need it.

65
00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:15.998
Bring your school-day map and move into a
group for your setting: a primary

66
00:05:15.999 --> 00:05:21.448
classroom, a secondary subject, or relief
or another setting. In the workshop room,

67
00:05:21.449 --> 00:05:26.832
use the three corners and form trios with
people nearby. If you don’t have a class

68
00:05:26.833 --> 00:05:32.017
yet, choose the setting you expect to work
in or used on placement. Each person

69
00:05:32.018 --> 00:05:37.002
needs an A three day-one setup sheet.
There are private My students cards on

70
00:05:37.003 --> 00:05:42.054
the second page; keep one for your own
check later. The same activity prompts

71
00:05:42.055 --> 00:05:47.106
are in the Participant Guide. If you are
following independently, choose your

72
00:05:47.107 --> 00:05:52.357
setting and work on your own setup. You
can ask a colleague to help you check it

73
00:05:52.358 --> 00:05:57.475
later. Take a few minutes to move, gather
your materials and settle. Pause the

74
00:05:57.476 --> 00:05:59.469
video now while you get ready.

75
00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:12.578
Let’s settle into the trios. Check that
each person has their own sheet and day

76
00:06:12.579 --> 00:06:17.611
map. Your setting gives you a useful
starting point for discussion. It doesn’t

77
00:06:17.612 --> 00:06:22.644
mean your classes or students have the
same needs. You’ll help each other make

78
00:06:22.645 --> 00:06:27.743
the plans specific and workable. Keep your
own two or three students in mind as

79
00:06:27.744 --> 00:06:28.330
we begin.

80
00:06:29.841 --> 00:06:35.127
Before we begin, bring a concise picture
of your students back into view. Include

81
00:06:35.128 --> 00:06:40.414
their strengths, interests and sources of
joy. Think about communication, sensory

82
00:06:40.415 --> 00:06:45.503
experience and thinking; what costs and
what lightens; identity and belonging.

83
00:06:45.504 --> 00:06:50.196
Include what students, families and the
team already know, alongside the

84
00:06:50.197 --> 00:06:55.152
questions still open. These are prompts to
hold in mind, not another form to

85
00:06:55.153 --> 00:07:00.373
complete. We’re not building a file on one
child, and we’re not ranking students

86
00:07:00.374 --> 00:07:05.396
by need. We’re keeping real, different
people in view as we design. Later, in

87
00:07:05.397 --> 00:07:10.551
the private check, you’ll return to each
of these students and consider how the

88
00:07:10.552 --> 00:07:13.260
same design may work differently for them.

89
00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:20.048
On section one of your A three sheet,
write a brief for your own day-one setup.

90
00:07:20.049 --> 00:07:24.826
You’ll also find the prompts on page
eighteen. First, note your setting:

91
00:07:24.827 --> 00:07:30.277
primary classroom, secondary subject, or
relief or another setting. Complete three

92
00:07:30.278 --> 00:07:35.661
sentences. On day one, my students will
need to. For some students, that means. I

93
00:07:35.662 --> 00:07:41.045
could set up. Draw on the school-day map
and choose a demand you can do something

94
00:07:41.046 --> 00:07:45.486
about. For example, in a primary classroom
you might be considering

95
00:07:45.487 --> 00:07:50.668
arrival: finding a place, settling and
understanding the morning routine. In a

96
00:07:50.669 --> 00:07:55.244
secondary lesson, you might be considering
how students enter, find a

97
00:07:55.245 --> 00:08:00.359
seat and understand what the lesson will
involve. These are possible starting

98
00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:04.868
points; use the demand in your own
setting. Spend about four minutes

99
00:08:04.869 --> 00:08:10.050
writing, then read your brief to your
trio. Help each other check the wording.

100
00:08:10.051 --> 00:08:14.559
Is it specific, workable and free from
blame or deficit labels? Keep

101
00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:19.000
observations separate from
interpretations, and mark what you still

102
00:08:19.001 --> 00:08:24.384
need to ask students or colleagues. Allow
six minutes altogether. Pause the video

103
00:08:24.385 --> 00:08:26.873
now for the brief and the trio check.

104
00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:39.533
Let’s come back to your three sentences.
Can you point to what students will need

105
00:08:39.534 --> 00:08:44.188
to access and what you could make clearer
or easier? Is the proposed setup within

106
00:08:44.189 --> 00:08:48.610
your influence? Keep any unanswered
question beside the brief. You can design

107
00:08:48.611 --> 00:08:52.799
a useful first step while still
recognising where consultation is needed.

108
00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:55.823
Leave the brief in front of you for the
options menu.

109
00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:02.652
We’ll consider four options: executive
functioning supports, predictability, a

110
00:09:02.653 --> 00:09:07.325
schedule you refer to, and visual
supports. Listen for the one or two

111
00:09:07.326 --> 00:09:12.549
options that answer your brief. The slide
refers to printed option cards, but

112
00:09:12.550 --> 00:09:17.636
separate cards have not been supplied with
these materials. You can use the

113
00:09:17.637 --> 00:09:23.134
four headings on screen and the Options I
chose section on page eighteen instead.

114
00:09:23.135 --> 00:09:28.702
Keep returning to your first sentence: on
day one, my students will need to. Which

115
00:09:28.703 --> 00:09:34.132
option could make that particular demand
more manageable? We’re matching support

116
00:09:34.133 --> 00:09:38.737
to a known demand, rather than collecting
a long list of strategies.

117
00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:44.915
Executive functioning supports can make
the work of starting, remembering,

118
00:09:44.916 --> 00:09:49.656
sequencing, organising and shifting easier
to manage. Start with the demand

119
00:09:49.657 --> 00:09:54.781
in your routine, then ask what would help
with that demand. If getting started is

120
00:09:54.782 --> 00:09:59.201
difficult, could the first step be visible
and clear? If the task asks

121
00:09:59.202 --> 00:10:04.262
students to hold several instructions in
mind, could the sequence stay available

122
00:10:04.263 --> 00:10:09.066
to return to? If organising time and
materials takes a great deal of effort,

123
00:10:09.067 --> 00:10:13.743
could the task make those easier to locate
and manage? If the challenge is

124
00:10:13.744 --> 00:10:18.996
moving between tasks, what information or
preparation would make the shift clearer?

125
00:10:18.997 --> 00:10:23.992
These are support examples to consider,
not a package every student needs. Look

126
00:10:23.993 --> 00:10:28.989
back at your day map and brief. Which
demand are you actually addressing? Would

127
00:10:28.990 --> 00:10:33.794
the support reduce unnecessary effort, or
add another thing the student must

128
00:10:33.795 --> 00:10:38.726
manage? Then consider how the student can
show or tell you whether it helps. A

129
00:10:38.727 --> 00:10:43.787
resource can look organised to an adult
and still be confusing or burdensome for

130
00:10:43.788 --> 00:10:48.720
its intended user. We’re looking for
better access and participation, with the

131
00:10:48.721 --> 00:10:53.588
student’s experience helping us judge
usefulness. More compliance pressure is

132
00:10:53.589 --> 00:10:54.357
not the aim.

133
00:10:55.880 --> 00:11:00.783
Predictability includes accessible
information about both routine and

134
00:11:00.784 --> 00:11:06.191
change. We can’t promise every day will be
the same. We can make information

135
00:11:06.192 --> 00:11:11.960
easier to find when a plan changes. Think
about how much classroom information is

136
00:11:11.961 --> 00:11:17.008
spoken once, implied, or kept in an
adult’s head. If I couldn’t rely on

137
00:11:17.009 --> 00:11:22.633
remembering what someone told me, how
would I know what was happening here? Use

138
00:11:22.634 --> 00:11:28.330
the four questions on the slide. What is
happening? What is changing? What comes

139
00:11:28.331 --> 00:11:34.099
next? What can I do if the plan changes?
Give that information early enough to be

140
00:11:34.100 --> 00:11:39.363
useful. In primary, perhaps assembly moves
earlier. In secondary, a lesson

141
00:11:39.364 --> 00:11:45.204
might move to another room or be taken by
a relief teacher. How will students find

142
00:11:45.205 --> 00:11:50.901
out? Is the change shown somewhere they
can return to? Can they see what follows

143
00:11:50.902 --> 00:11:56.670
and ask a question or request support in a
way that works for them? A new teacher

144
00:11:56.671 --> 00:12:01.862
is already a change for students.
Explaining how the class will run helps

145
00:12:01.863 --> 00:12:07.342
from the first lesson. Return to your day
map and brief: where could you make

146
00:12:07.343 --> 00:12:11.019
information about routine and change more
available?

147
00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:17.697
A schedule makes sequence, timing and
change visible. It can cover a first-then

148
00:12:17.698 --> 00:12:22.940
sequence, a single lesson, part of the day
or the whole day. Choose a format the

149
00:12:22.941 --> 00:12:27.852
intended students can understand and
access. There’s a difference between a

150
00:12:27.853 --> 00:12:32.897
schedule that is displayed and one that is
used. Refer to it at the start, at

151
00:12:32.898 --> 00:12:37.809
changes of activity and when plans change.
A primary classroom might have a

152
00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:42.655
whole-day schedule by the door. In
secondary or relief teaching, you might

153
00:12:42.656 --> 00:12:47.700
put the lesson plan on the board and refer
back to it as you move through the

154
00:12:47.701 --> 00:12:52.944
lesson. Check whether the schedule shows
students’ actual day, including changes

155
00:12:52.945 --> 00:12:58.187
and opportunities to pause. If it only
lists the tasks adults want completed, it

156
00:12:58.188 --> 00:13:03.564
may leave important questions unanswered.
The example image on this slide is still

157
00:13:03.565 --> 00:13:08.543
a placeholder in the supplied deck. As you
consider an example from your own

158
00:13:08.544 --> 00:13:13.720
setting, ask the question at the bottom:
does the schedule still provide useful

159
00:13:13.721 --> 00:13:18.898
information when the day changes? Its
value comes from reducing uncertainty and

160
00:13:18.899 --> 00:13:23.876
supporting participation. Watch it in use,
invite feedback, and adjust it as

161
00:13:23.877 --> 00:13:25.801
you learn what students need.

162
00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:31.993
With any visual support, start by asking
what the student needs to find,

163
00:13:31.994 --> 00:13:36.799
understand, remember or communicate. Then
choose a form that can make that

164
00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:41.671
information clear. It might be an object,
photograph, symbol, written word,

165
00:13:41.672 --> 00:13:46.873
checklist, map or choice support. Visual
does not automatically mean accessible.

166
00:13:46.874 --> 00:13:52.008
The student needs to understand what it
represents and be able to use it at the

167
00:13:52.009 --> 00:13:57.143
point of need. Keep unnecessary detail
out, explain how it works, and check its

168
00:13:57.144 --> 00:14:02.212
meaning with the student. Page nineteen
gives four quick-review questions. Can

169
00:14:02.213 --> 00:14:06.887
the student understand it? Can they access
it when they need it? Does it

170
00:14:06.888 --> 00:14:11.627
reflect what is actually happening,
including change? Does it give useful

171
00:14:11.628 --> 00:14:16.631
information or a way to respond? Keep
understanding and expressing in view. A

172
00:14:16.632 --> 00:14:21.305
visual support may help someone
understand, while A A C provides ways to

173
00:14:21.306 --> 00:14:26.572
communicate. Access to A A C doesn’t mean
every spoken instruction or schedule is

174
00:14:26.573 --> 00:14:30.983
understood. Keep the student’s
communication system available during

175
00:14:30.984 --> 00:14:35.987
routines, transitions and changes, and
recognise responses beyond speech. The

176
00:14:35.988 --> 00:14:40.661
materials refer to a Visual Supports
Checklist and an online webinar for

177
00:14:40.662 --> 00:14:45.467
further learning, but their access details
still need to be confirmed. The

178
00:14:45.468 --> 00:14:50.668
review questions here are ready to use. A
checklist supports personalisation and

179
00:14:50.669 --> 00:14:55.738
consultation; it does not prescribe one
format for every student. Keep asking:

180
00:14:55.739 --> 00:15:00.544
does this give this student useful
information they can understand, access

181
00:15:00.545 --> 00:15:01.267
and act on?

182
00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:07.960
Return to your brief. Which one or two
options answer it? Look at executive

183
00:15:07.961 --> 00:15:12.911
functioning supports, predictability, a
schedule you refer to and visual

184
00:15:12.912 --> 00:15:18.211
supports. For each option you choose,
write why it fits: which demand in your

185
00:15:18.212 --> 00:15:23.651
classroom does it answer? Record your
choice and reason on section two of the A

186
00:15:23.652 --> 00:15:28.811
three sheet, or on page eighteen of the
Participant Guide. If all four look

187
00:15:28.812 --> 00:15:33.693
useful, narrow your choice to what matters
most for this setup. You can

188
00:15:33.694 --> 00:15:39.412
return to other possibilities later. Take
about two minutes to decide, with a brief

189
00:15:39.413 --> 00:15:44.641
conversation in your trio if useful. Pause
here while you record the choice.

190
00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:57.706
Check that each option has a reason beside
it. What specifically will it make

191
00:15:57.707 --> 00:16:03.064
clearer, easier to find or more workable
in your setting? Keep that match visible.

192
00:16:03.065 --> 00:16:08.224
It will help you explain the setup and
decide what to observe when students use

193
00:16:08.225 --> 00:16:08.422
it.

194
00:16:09.960 --> 00:16:15.180
Before you finish the design, let’s apply
a Universal Design for Learning lens. Can

195
00:16:15.181 --> 00:16:20.338
you make important information clear and
available? Can you offer workable ways to

196
00:16:20.339 --> 00:16:25.113
participate and respond? Can you support
engagement while keeping meaningful

197
00:16:25.114 --> 00:16:30.143
challenge in the learning? Knowledge of
individual students and universal design

198
00:16:30.144 --> 00:16:35.046
can inform each other. Something you
understand about one student’s access may

199
00:16:35.047 --> 00:16:39.885
reveal a barrier worth reducing more
broadly. A broadly available support may

200
00:16:39.886 --> 00:16:44.660
then help several students in different
ways. But the presence of a resource

201
00:16:44.661 --> 00:16:49.436
doesn’t guarantee access. Offering another
format only helps if students can

202
00:16:49.437 --> 00:16:54.339
find it, understand it and use it. We’ll
now examine your emerging design with

203
00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:58.350
four practical questions, then test it
through use and feedback.

204
00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:04.743
Use section three of your A three sheet,
with the prompts on page nineteen. Each

205
00:17:04.744 --> 00:17:09.730
person will run four questions over their
own draft setup, with their trio’s help.

206
00:17:09.731 --> 00:17:14.286
Can students find and understand what they
need? Can they take part in more

